Manual review corrections for this page's first pass.

- Every vector variable ($\text{v}$, $\text{n}$, $\text{i}$, $\text{j}$,
  $\text{k}$, $\text{r}$, and $\text{s}$) had been converted to upright,
  non-bold \text{} instead of boldface, matching the same defect already
  fixed for vol3-2-1-vectors-in-the-plane.tex,
  vol3-2-2-vectors-in-three-dimensions.tex,
  vol3-2-3-the-dot-product.tex, and
  vol3-2-4-the-cross-product.tex, and unlike every other
  vector-notation exercise file in this repo and OpenStax's own boldface
  vector convention; changed every such occurrence to \mathbf{}.
- Five occurrences joined the last item of a comma-separated list of
  points into the same math span as the word "and" via \text{and} plus
  manual \, spacing (e.g. "$P,Q,\,\text{and}\,R$", "$a,b,\,\text{and}\,c$"),
  the pattern this skill flags against (English glued into math mode
  instead of plain-text "and" between separate math spans); split each
  into separate math spans joined by plain-text "and" (e.g. "$P$, $Q$,
  and $R$").
- Bare "x-axis" and "xy-plane" appeared as plain prose instead of the
  file's own convention of typesetting axis/plane names with the variable
  in math mode (matching the convention already established across
  reviewed files, e.g. "$x$-axis", "$xy$-plane"); corrected the three
  occurrences.
- "Find point P of tangency." and "the components of r are expressed in
  centimeters" left the variables P and r as bare, non-mathematical prose
  instead of the math-mode variables used consistently for the same
  quantities elsewhere in the same file and sentence; wrapped them in math
  mode as $P$ and $\mathbf{r}$.
- "$x\,=\,3\,+\,3t,\,y\,=\,1\,+\,8t,\,z\,=\,6t$" used erratic manual \,
  spacing around every operator, unlike every sibling parametric-equation
  triple in the file (e.g. "$x=1+t,y=3+t,z=5+4t$"); removed the stray \,
  to match.
- "$P(4,0,5),Q(2,3,1)$" was missing the comma-and-space separator used
  between every other P/Q coordinate pair in the same list; corrected to
  "$P(4,0,5)$, $Q(2,3,1)$".

